\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Cyclic arc precision on the dyadic index}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{For each integer #{k\ge 0}, write
#{X_k=\mathbb Z/2^k\mathbb Z}. The doubling map
#{X_k\to X_{k+1}} sends the class of an integer #{j} to the
class of #{2j}. These maps form an inductive system. Its direct
limit is the \newvocab{dyadic index} #{D}. The class of #{j} at
level #{k} is the point #{j/2^k}, read modulo #{1}. The next
level represents the same point, because
#{(2j)/2^{k+1}=j/2^k}.}

\p{Let #{S\subset D} be finite, with #{|S|\ge 2}, and equip
#{\mathbb R^S} with its
\vocabk{counting inner products}{fdip-0002}. List the points of
#{S} in the cyclic order of their positions modulo #{1}, and
let #{h_i>0} be the oriented arc from its source #{s} to the next
point #{t}, taken once. Each arc
is a difference of dyadic positions. Viewing the same points at
a finer level does not change the arcs, and the arcs sum to
#{1}. Fix #{m>0}. On #{u,v\in\mathbb R^S}, that arc contributes
the symmetric bilinear expression
##{m\coth(mh_i)(u_s v_s+u_t v_t)
-m\operatorname{csch}(mh_i)(u_s v_t+u_t v_s).}
The \newvocab{cyclic arc precision} #{Q_S} is the
counting-metric Riesz operator of the sum of these expressions
over the oriented cyclic arcs: the counting inner product of
#{u} with #{Q_Sv} equals that sum. When #{|S|=2}, the arcs #{h}
and #{1-h} are parallel edges between the same two points, and
both are included in the sum, with no factor #{\frac{1}{2}}.}

\p{Remark. Declare the counting basis of site indicators in the
cyclic order. In that basis, when #{|S|\ge 3},
##{Q_{ii}=m\bigl(\coth(m h_{i-1})+\coth(m h_i)\bigr),
\qquad
Q_{i,i+1}=-\frac{m}{\sinh(m h_i)},}
with indices read in that cyclic order, and with every other
entry equal to #{0}. When #{|S|=2}, the arcs #{h} and #{1-h}
are parallel edges between the same two points, so the couplings
add: both diagonal entries equal
#{m\bigl(\coth(m h)+\coth(m(1-h))\bigr)}, and both
off-diagonal entries equal
#{-m\left(\frac{1}{\sinh(m h)}+\frac{1}{\sinh(m(1-h))}\right)}.}
